@meta
id: 17-12
title: A mark you named yourself
chapter: 17
type: lesson
skills: marks-set, marks-jump, mark-backtick
par: 104
minutes: 7
vscode: a bookmark extension, if you installed one
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Three review comments on this provider. The first one is on the
`emailService.send` call in `create`: it is not awaited. Search for
`this.email` to land on it. You will fix it last.

The other two are elsewhere. In `cancel`, `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED`
should be `ORDER_NOT_CANCELLABLE`. In `refundPartial`,
`REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL` should be `REFUND_TOO_LARGE`. Fix both, in that
order.

Then come back to the first comment and insert `await ` in front of
`this`. You have used `Ctrl-O` for this before, and you could count
your jumps and use it again. Instead, before you leave, put a mark on
the exact character you will need to be on when you return. Then the
number of jumps in between is nobody's problem.

@keys
| `ma` | set mark `a` at the cursor position |
| `` `a `` | jump to mark `a`, exact character |
| `'a` | jump to the line of mark `a`, first non-blank |
| `:marks` | list every mark that is set |

@hints
Set the mark while the cursor is on the `t` of `this`, which is where
the search leaves it. That is the character `await ` goes in front of.
--
The backtick form returns you to the character; the apostrophe form
only to the line. After `'a` you would still have to find `this`.
--
`/this.email` Enter, `ma`. `/ALREADY` Enter, `ciwORDER_NOT_CANCELLABLE`
Esc. `/EXCEEDS` Enter, `ciwREFUND_TOO_LARGE` Esc. `` `a ``, `iawait `
Esc.

@solution
    /this.email<Enter> ma
    /ALREADY<Enter> ciwORDER_NOT_CANCELLABLE<Esc>
    /EXCEEDS<Enter> ciwREFUND_TOO_LARGE<Esc>
    `a iawait <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Chapter 9 gave you `ma` and `'a` as a way to say "from here to there"
for an operator. This is the other use, and the more common one: a
bookmark. Drop it before you leave, jump back to it whenever you like.

Compared with `Ctrl-O`, a mark has three properties worth having. It
does not care how many jumps you made in between, so there is nothing
to count. It does not move when you make other jumps, so it is still
right after a long detour. And it has a name, so you can keep several:
`ma` on the thing you are fixing, `mb` on the test for it, `mc` on the
type definition, and hop between all three for as long as the file is
open.

`` `a `` versus `'a` is the backtick rule again. The apostrophe version
lands on the first non-blank of the line, which here is the `a` of
`await`'s future neighbour, four characters short of where you need
to be. Use the backtick by default. Use the apostrophe when you mean
whole lines, which mostly means when you are giving the mark to an
operator, `d'a`, or to an Ex range, `:'a,.s`.

`:marks` lists everything: your lowercase marks, and the automatic
ones from the last few lessons. Look at it once to see that `.`, `^`,
`[` and `]` are marks like any other.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    await this.customerProvider.findOne(customerId);

    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }

  public async create(args: { customerId: string; total: number }) {
    const customer = await this.customerProvider.findOne(args.customerId);

    const order = await this.orderRepository.create({
      values: { customerId: customer.id, total: args.total, status: 'PENDING' },
    });

    this.emailService.send({ orderId: order.id, template: 'order_confirmation' });

    return order;
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PENDING');
    }

    await this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async refundPartial(id: string, amount: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (amount > order.total) {
      throw exception('REFUND_EXCEEDS_TOTAL');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { refundedAmount: amount },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerProvider } from '../customer/customer.provider';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerProvider) protected customerProvider: CustomerProvider,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    await this.customerProvider.findOne(customerId);

    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }

  public async create(args: { customerId: string; total: number }) {
    const customer = await this.customerProvider.findOne(args.customerId);

    const order = await this.orderRepository.create({
      values: { customerId: customer.id, total: args.total, status: 'PENDING' },
    });

    await this.emailService.send({ orderId: order.id, template: 'order_confirmation' });

    return order;
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_PENDING');
    }

    await this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_CANCELLABLE');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async refundPartial(id: string, amount: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (amount > order.total) {
      throw exception('REFUND_TOO_LARGE');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id, amount);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { refundedAmount: amount },
    });
  }
}
